; wspr_tx.loom
; A WSPR beacon source: for each message the host queues (callsign,
; four-character locator, power in dBm) the program packs the 50 source
; bits, codes them with WSPR's rate 1/2, constraint-length 32
; convolutional code, interleaves the 162 coded bits by bit reversal,
; merges them with the 162-bit sync vector and sends the 162 four-level
; symbols as a phase-continuous 4-FSK square wave at an audio offset, for
; the audio input of an SSB transmitter, with a push-to-talk line around it.
;
; Written on 2026-11-07, after the hardware was complete (D-032), as a
; protocol Loom was not designed for (D-035). Nothing in Loom was built for
; WSPR: there is no multiplier, no 32-bit register, no oscillator. The
; program uses
;   - the per-thread tick (TICK_INT/TICK_FRAC, built for UART baud rates)
;     as the 96 kHz sample clock of a numerically controlled oscillator;
;   - the deadline schedule (WAITD 1 every sample, proved by the checker)
;     and the deadline-latched SETP (D-016, built for clock-exact line-code
;     edges) for the push-to-talk edges, which land on the exact tick;
;   - OUT through a two-pin OUTGRP group (built for parallel buses), so the
;     tone bit and the symbol marker reach their pins at the same edge;
;   - REV (built for LSB/MSB-first conversions) for the 8-bit address
;     reversal of the interleaver, and PAR (built for parity bits) for the
;     parity of each 16-bit half of the AND of register and polynomial;
;   - the data memory (LD/ST, built for device-emulation tables) for the
;     sync vector, the tone table, the packed message and the interleaved
;     bits.
; What the firmware does that no hardware does: the character mapping and
; callsign alignment, the source packing (N, 28 bits, and M, 22 bits, by a
; 16 x 16 shift-and-add multiply in a subroutine), a 32-bit shift register
; and its two 32-bit tap masks in pairs of 16-bit words, the interleaver,
; the sync merge, the tone table and the symbol timing.
;
; Sources (tools/protomodels/wspr.py cites them in full): A. Talbot G4JNT,
; "The WSPR Coding Process" (June 2009), a description of K1JT's WSPR
; encoder: the packing formulas, the polynomials 0xF2D05351 and
; 0xE4613C47, the bit-reversal interleaver, the sync vector, Symbol[n] =
; Sync[n] + 2 Data[n], and the tone spacing of 12000 / 8192 Hz with a
; symbol length of its reciprocal (8192 / 12000 s, 162 symbols in 110.6 s).
;
; Sample clock, tones and their error. The tick is the sample period; at
; fs = 96 000 Hz the 16-bit phase accumulator's step 1 is fs / 65536 =
; 1.46484375 Hz = 12000 / 8192 Hz, exactly WSPR's tone spacing, and one
; symbol is 8192 / 12000 s x 96 000 = 65 536 samples exactly. So the host's
; real settings are base step B (the audio offset is B x 1.46484375 Hz:
; B = 1024 is 1500.000 Hz, WSPR's 200 Hz window 1400..1600 Hz is B = 956
; .. 1092), spacing step 1 and 65 536 samples a symbol (written 0). Tone k
; is step B + k, and the top bit of the accumulator is a square wave whose
; mean frequency is (B + k) x fs / 65536 exactly; the phase is continuous
; across symbols. The frequency resolution is therefore 1.4648 Hz for the
; offset and zero for the spacing; the only errors are the board clock's
; (e ppm moves every tone and the symbol length by e ppm) and the tick's
; rounding: at 50 MHz the tick is 50e6 / 96000 = 520.833 clocks, set as
; TICK_INT = 520, TICK_FRAC = 213 (520.832), +2.6 ppm, 0.004 Hz at 1500 Hz.
; Edges sit on the sample grid, so each half-period is a whole number of
; samples: the cycle-to-cycle jitter is one sample (10.4 us), the mean is
; exact. Harmonics of a 1.4..1.6 kHz square wave start at 4.2 kHz, outside
; an SSB transmitter's audio passband; the transmitter's filter, not
; Loom, makes it a sine.
;
; Scaled timing for simulation (how the tests run it). Every interval is
; counted in ticks: samples a symbol is a host word, so a run at 16 to 64
; samples a symbol and spacing steps of 512 to 2048 (tones far enough apart
; to tell in a few cycles) is the same program, word for word, at a few
; thousand times the speed. The tests say which settings they use.
;
; Contract on the pins (TONE = OUT0, SYM = OUT1, the next index after TONE,
; PTT = OUT2; any outputs with --pin, SYM always at TONE + 1). Sample n of a
; transmission (n = 0 at the tick where PTT rises) shows on TONE bit 15 of
; the phase after the steps of samples 0 .. n-1 (the phase starts at 0),
; each sample of symbol j adding the step of symbol j's tone; samples
; j L .. j L + L - 1 belong to symbol j (L samples a symbol). SYM is high
; during even symbols and low during odd ones, so it rises with PTT and
; changes at every symbol boundary. PTT rises on the exact tick of sample
; 0 and falls on the exact tick of sample 162 L (latched SETP); TONE and SYM
; change one slot after their tick (4 to 7 clocks).
;
; Host interface (FIFO space, thread 0):
;   INQ, once after start: the base step B, the spacing step S, samples a
;     symbol L (0 means 65 536);
;   INQ, then, per message, eleven words: the callsign's six characters,
;     ASCII upper case, left-justified and padded with spaces as written
;     ("G4JNT "; the program inserts the leading space that puts a digit in
;     the third place, as the WSPR rules ask), the locator's four
;     characters ("IO83"), and the power in dBm (0..60).
;   The transmission starts when the eleventh word arrives (encoding takes
;   about 34 000 clocks, 0.7 ms at 50 MHz); WSPR's start, one second into an even UTC
;   minute, is the host's to keep. Messages queue: the next one is encoded
;   right after PTT falls. Nothing goes to OUTQ. Input is not validated:
;   characters other than A-Z, 0-9 and space give a wrong message.
;
; The 16-bit arithmetic. N needs 28 bits and M 22: N is built by Horner's
; rule on a 32-bit pair r3:r4 with mac (r3:r4 = r3:r4 x R + c by R - 1
; 32-bit additions, the carry taken with BNC/INC: Loom has no add-with-
; carry and no multiplier); M1 and its products stay below 2^16. The
; 32-bit shift register is r0:r1, shifted with SHLI and the carry moved by
; hand; the parity of (register AND polynomial) is PAR of the XOR of the
; two 16-bit ANDs. The interleaver walks I = 0..255, J = REV(I) >> 8, and
; produces coded bit P only when J < 162, so the coding and the
; interleaving are one loop and no array of the 162 coded bits exists.
;
; Memory: 0x000 .. 0x0F7 (248 words: 226 emitted, the rest data written
; before it is read: the tone steps, L, the packed message, the
; interleaved bits, which also hold the characters while they are
; packed). That is thread 0's quarter and 120 words of thread 1's (.imem
; 512), so the program runs as thread 0, alone or with programs in
; threads 2 and 3; thread 1 must not be started. The data is reached by
; absolute imm5 offsets below 32 (only r0 = 0 or an index as base), so
; the program cannot move to another thread's quarter.
;
; Deadlines: the longest path from one WAITD 1 to the next is the last
; sample of a symbol, which sets up the next, 36 slots, 144 clocks; the
; checker proves every deadline at 144 clocks. The PTT edge needs 5 more:
; the staged SETP lands on the exact tick only if that WAITD's first issue
; commits by the tick (SEMANTICS 6.10 rule 1), so 4 x 36 + 2 + 3 (the
; previous WAITD's lag) = 149 clocks. The program declares 152, 14.6 MHz
; at 96 kHz; at 50 MHz the tick is 520.83 clocks.
;
; Readings taken (QUESTIONS.md): type 1 messages only (no compound
; callsigns, no six-character locators: those are type 2 and 3 messages
; and need a second transmission); the third-character rule implemented
; as "if the third character is not a digit, prefix a space"; the audio
; square wave is the output (a transmitter filters it), no PWM sine.
;
; Readings taken on the open spec questions (2026-11-07): coding from
; G4JNT's "The WSPR Coding Process" (secondary to K1JT's code). The
; encoder matches two published 162-symbol lists (G1ABC IO83 37 and
; KA1BCD AA00 33) from an independent implementation of that
; description; K1JT's own encoder output was not checked.
;   Type 1 messages only. The callsign alignment rule is a reading of
;   G4JNT's "if necessary".
;   The output is a square wave at the audio offset, not a sine: it
;   needs a filter before any transmitter.

        .imem   512
        .thread 0
        .pins   TONE = OUT0, SYM = OUT1, PTT = OUT2
        .tick   152                     ; 144 proves every deadline; PTT needs 149
        .equ    SYMLEN = 1              ; samples a symbol (0 = 65 536)
        .equ    TONES = 2               ; 4 words: the step of tone 0..3
        .equ    MSG = 6                 ; 6 words: the 81 bits, MSB first
        .equ    DATA = 12               ; 11 words: the interleaved bits
        .equ    CS = DATA               ; 10 words: character values, read
        .equ    LOC = CS + 6            ;   before DATA is written
        .equ    SYNC = 23               ; 11 words: the sync vector
        .equ    CODE = 34
        .equ    NSYM = 162

        JMP     start
; The sync vector (G4JNT, "Merge With Sync Vector"), bit j of the vector
; in bit (j mod 16) of word j / 16: LSB first, as DATA is. Every other
; word below CODE is stored before it is read, so the image leaves them.
        .org    SYNC
        .word   0x7103, 0x07A4, 0x40A4, 0x58B3, 0x9558, 0x5634, 0xC904, 0xE2CD
        .word   0x0CA0, 0x6358, 0x0000

; ---------------------------------------------------------------- set-up
        .org    CODE
start:  .csr    OUTGRP, (2 << 5) | pin(TONE)    ; OUT r: r[0] TONE, r[1] SYM
        LDI     r0, 0
        OUT     r0
        SETP    PTT, 0
        POP     r1                      ; B, the base step (blocks)
        POP     r2                      ; S, the spacing step
        POP     r3                      ; L, samples a symbol
        ST      r3, r0, SYMLEN
        ST      r1, r0, TONES           ; tone k: B + k S
        ADD     r1, r1, r2
        ST      r1, r0, TONES + 1
        ADD     r1, r1, r2
        ST      r1, r0, TONES + 2
        ADD     r1, r1, r2
        ST      r1, r0, TONES + 3

; ------------------------------------------------ a message: 11 words
; r0 = 0 (the base of every absolute LD/ST until the encoder)
msg:    LDI     r0, 0
        LDI     r6, 0
getc:   POP     r3                      ; ASCII (blocks)
        SUBI    r3, '0'
        BNC     space                   ; below '0': the space
        CMPI    r3, 10
        BNC     put                     ; '0'..'9': 0..9
        SUBI    r3, 'A' - '0' - 10      ; 'A'..'Z': 10..35
        BRA     put
space:  LDI     r3, 36
put:    ST      r3, r6, CS              ; CS[0..5] callsign, LOC[0..3]
        INC     r6
        CMPI    r6, 10
        BNZ     getc
        LD      r3, r0, CS + 2          ; a digit third? else shift the
        CMPI    r3, 10                  ; callsign right by one and put
        BNC     packn                   ; a space in front (" G4JNT")
        LDI     r6, 5
shift:  LD      r3, r6, CS - 1
        ST      r3, r6, CS
        DJNZ    r6, shift
        LDI     r3, 36
        ST      r3, r0, CS

; N by G4JNT's N1..N6: N = N x R + c for the six characters, R = 36, 10,
; 27, 27, 27 (the first R is any: N starts at 0), c - 10 for the last
; three. r3:r4 = N, r7 = the character. mac: r3:r4 = r3:r4 x r2 + r1.
packn:  LDI     r3, 0
        LDI     r4, 0
        LDI     r7, 0
nchar:  LD      r1, r7, CS
        LDI     r2, 36
        CMPI    r7, 2
        BNC     nmac                    ; characters 0, 1
        LDI     r2, 10
        BZ      nmac                    ; character 2 (Z of the CMPI)
        LDI     r2, 27
        SUBI    r1, 10                  ; characters 3..5
nmac:   CALL    mac
        INC     r7
        CMPI    r7, 6
        BNZ     nchar
        MOV     r1, r4                  ; MSG[0] = N[27:12]
        SHRI    r1, 12
        SHLI    r3, 4
        OR      r3, r3, r1
        ST      r3, r0, MSG
        SHLI    r4, 4                   ; MSG[1] = N[11:0], M[21:18]
        ST      r4, r0, MSG + 1

; M1 = (179 - 10 Loc1 - Loc3) x 180 + 10 Loc2 + Loc4, M = M1 x 128 + dBm
; + 64 (22 bits). The letters arrive as 10..27 and lose their 10 here.
; Every product is below 2^16, so r3 stays 0.
        LD      r4, r0, LOC + 1
        SUBI    r4, 10
        LD      r1, r0, LOC + 3
        LDI     r2, 10
        CALL    mac                     ; 10 Loc2 + Loc4
        ST      r4, r0, MSG + 2
        LD      r4, r0, LOC
        SUBI    r4, 10
        LD      r1, r0, LOC + 2
        LDI     r2, 10
        CALL    mac                     ; 10 Loc1 + Loc3
        LDI     r1, 179
        SUB     r4, r1, r4
        LD      r1, r0, MSG + 2
        LDI     r2, 180
        CALL    mac                     ; M1 (<= 32 220)
        POP     r7                      ; the power, dBm (the eleventh word)
        MOV     r1, r4
        SHRI    r1, 9                   ; r1 = M[21:16]
        SHLI    r4, 7
        OR      r4, r4, r7
        LDI     r2, 64
        OR      r4, r4, r2              ; r4 = M[15:0]
        MOV     r2, r1                  ; MSG[1] |= M[21:18]
        SHRI    r2, 2
        LD      r3, r0, MSG + 1
        OR      r3, r3, r2
        ST      r3, r0, MSG + 1
        SHLI    r1, 14                  ; MSG[2] = M[17:2]
        MOV     r2, r4
        SHRI    r2, 2
        OR      r1, r1, r2
        ST      r1, r0, MSG + 2
        SHLI    r4, 14                  ; MSG[3] = M[1:0], then zeros
        ST      r4, r0, MSG + 3
        ST      r0, r0, MSG + 4
        ST      r0, r0, MSG + 5
        LDI     r6, 11                  ; DATA = 0 (it held CS and LOC)
clear:  ST      r0, r6, DATA - 1
        DJNZ    r6, clear

; ----------------------------------------- coding and interleaving at once
; For I = 0..255, J = rev8(I); when J < 162 the next coded bit S[P] is
; made and stored at D[J] (G4JNT "Interleaving"). Coded bit P: for even P
; message bit P / 2 enters the register r0:r1 and the parity is with
; 0xF2D05351, for odd P with 0xE4613C47. r5 = I, r6 = P, r7 the message
; word being read, MSB first.
        LDI     r0, 0
        LDI     r1, 0
        LDI     r5, 0
        LDI     r6, 0
enc:    MOV     r2, r5
        REV     r2, r2
        SHRI    r2, 8                   ; J
        MOV16   r3, NSYM
        CMP     r2, r3
        BC      next                    ; J >= 162: no bit
        MOV     r3, r6
        ANDI    r3, 1
        BNZ     odd
        MOV     r3, r6                  ; every 16 message bits (P mod 32
        ANDI    r3, 31                  ; = 0) the next MSG word
        BNZ     shin
        MOV     r3, r6
        SHRI    r3, 5
        LD      r7, r3, MSG
shin:   SHLI    r0, 1                   ; register <<= 1
        SHLI    r1, 1
        BNC     1
        ORI     r0, 1
        SHLI    r7, 1                   ; + message bit P / 2
        BNC     1
        ORI     r1, 1
        MOV16   r3, 0xF2D0
        MOV16   r4, 0x5351
        BRA     par
odd:    MOV16   r3, 0xE461
        MOV16   r4, 0x3C47
par:    INC     r6
        AND     r3, r3, r0
        AND     r4, r4, r1
        XOR     r3, r3, r4
        PAR     r3, r3                  ; C = the coded bit
        BNC     next                    ; DATA is clear: a 0 is stored
        MOV     r3, r2
        ANDI    r3, 15
        LDI     r4, 1
        SHL     r4, r4, r3
        SHRI    r2, 4
        LD      r3, r2, DATA
        OR      r3, r3, r4
        ST      r3, r2, DATA
next:   INC     r5
        MOV     r3, r5
        SHRI    r3, 8
        BZ      enc

; ------------------------------------------------------------ transmit
; r0 phase   r1 the symbol's step   r2 J, the symbol   r4 SYM in bit 1
; r5 the next TONE/SYM bits   r6 samples left in the symbol   r7 L
        LDI     r0, 0
        LD      r7, r0, SYMLEN
        LDI     r1, 0                   ; a silent sample first ("symbol
        LDI     r4, 0                   ; -1", one sample long, step 0,
        LDI     r5, 0                   ; pins low) so that symbol 0 is
        LDI     r6, 1                   ; set up by newsym like any other
        LDI     r2, 0
        DEC     r2
        SETD    1

; One sample: the bits decided last sample go out right after the tick,
; then the phase advances by the symbol's step. 6 slots and the WAITD.
loop:   WAITD   1
        OUT     r5
        ADD     r0, r0, r1
        MOV     r5, r0
        SHRI    r5, 15                  ; TONE = bit 15 of the phase
        OR      r5, r5, r4
        DJNZ    r6, loop
; The last sample of symbol J has been computed: set up symbol J + 1 in
; the same sample period, so the sample grid never slips. Symbol J =
; Sync[J] + 2 Data[J]; r1 = its tone's step, r4 = SYM (high for even J).
newsym: INC     r2
        MOV16   r3, NSYM
        CMP     r2, r3
        BC      done
        SETP    PTT, 1, D               ; lands on the symbol's first tick
        MOV     r3, r2
        ANDI    r3, 15
        MOV     r4, r2
        SHRI    r4, 4
        LD      r1, r4, DATA
        LD      r4, r4, SYNC
        SHR     r1, r1, r3
        ANDI    r1, 1
        SHLI    r1, 1
        SHR     r4, r4, r3
        ANDI    r4, 1
        OR      r1, r1, r4
        LD      r1, r1, TONES
        MOV     r4, r2
        ANDI    r4, 1
        XORI    r4, 1
        SHLI    r4, 1
        MOV     r6, r7
        ANDI    r5, 1                   ; the new symbol's SYM level
        OR      r5, r5, r4
        BRA     loop
done:   SETP    PTT, 0, D               ; lands on sample 162 L's tick
        WAITD   1
        LDI     r5, 0                   ; TONE and SYM low
        OUT     r5
        BRA     msg

; mac: r3:r4 = r3:r4 x r2 + r1 for r2 >= 1, by r2 - 1 additions of the
; 32-bit value to its own sum with r1. r2, r5 and r6 are destroyed.
mac:    MOV     r5, r3
        MOV     r6, r4
        ADD     r4, r4, r1
        BNC     1
        INC     r3
mloop:  DJNZ    r2, madd
        RET
madd:   ADD     r4, r4, r6
        BNC     1
        INC     r3
        ADD     r3, r3, r5
        BRA     mloop
